---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
title: "Getting Started";
description: "A quick introduction to using Umi‑Time — a planetary way to see the UTC day.";
---

<Base title="Getting Started with Umi-Time">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <section class="page">
      <h1>Getting Started</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Umi‑Time gives you a single, planetary time of day — the same
        everywhere, interpreted locally. This page helps you see it, understand
        it, and use it within a minute.
      </p>

      <!-- 1. What You Can Do Here -->
      <section>
        <h2>What You Can Do Here</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>View Umi‑Time in your browser</li>
          <li>Understand how it relates to civil time and UTC</li>
          <li>Use the interactive clock</li>
          <li>Install the library for your own projects</li>
          <li>Convert between Umi‑Time and local time</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. Clock Demo -->
      <section>
        <h2>See Umi‑Time in Action</h2>
        <p>
          The Umi‑Time analog clock shows the UTC day as a single continuous rotation —
          the simplest way to understand the system.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a class="button" href="/clock">Open the Clock Demo</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Understanding the Basics -->
      <section>
        <h2>Understanding the Basics</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time expresses the day as a smooth, uninterrupted 360‑unit cycle
          derived directly from UTC. Every place on Earth shares the same umi
          value at the same moment, with local meaning provided by the
          orientation of the dial.
        </p>

        <p>
          Umi‑Time builds on UTC as with presentational layers. The first layer
          provides local meaning, mapping the global umi value to where it
          occurs in the observer's day. A second layer provides even greater
          local context by representing solar events, like sunrise and sunset,
          at the umi positions where they occur.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Using Umi‑Time Day to Day -->
      <section>
        <h2>Using Umi‑Time Day to Day</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time helps you understand where the world is in the day at a
          glance. It simplifies global coordination, clarifies the structure of
          the UTC day, and provides a shared frame for events, travel, and
          communication.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Developer Quickstart -->
      <section>
        <h2>Developer Quickstart</h2>
        <p>
          If you want to use Umi‑Time programmatically, here is a simple
          example:
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`// Get the current Umi‑Time value
import { UmiTime } from "umi-time";
import { UMI_EXTENDED } from "umi-time";

const now = new Date();
const umi = new UmiTime(now);

const text = umi.format(UMI_EXTENDED);
console.log(text);
`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />

        <p>
          See the <a href="/docs/api">Developer API Reference</a> for full details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Next Steps -->
      <section>
        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/faq">FAQ</a></li>
          <li><a href="/design">Design Principles</a></li>
          <li><a href="/api">Developer API Reference</a></li>
          <li><a href="/demos">Examples & Demos</a></li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/umi-time" target="_blank">GitHub</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
